00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.840 align:start position:0%
European Union transport ministers have  agreed on a proposal to increase the  availability of alternative fuels across  the EU to help stimulate demand for  clean power the aim is to reduce  Europe's dependence on fossil fuels  improve energy security and cut  greenhouse gas emissions from sources  like Motor Vehicles this would be done  in part by setting a minimum number of  recharge points for electric cars in EU  countries and outlets for natural gas  and hydrogen negotiations on the  proposals are soon to be launched with  the European Parliament it's hoped that  this infrastructure could be in place by  2030 the ministers also took stock of  progress made on improving air passenger  rights the Commission's proposed ways to  clear up gray areas on rules concerning  the rights of people whose flights are  delayed or cancelled and to boost  complaint handling regulations the goal  is to strike a balance between passenger  rights and avoiding high costs for  airlines meanwhile ministers responsible  for telecommunications held a first  policy debate on ways to limit mobile  telephone roaming charges and barriers  to service providers doing business  across the EU The ministers welcomed the  aims of the proposals on updating  Europe's telecommunications regulatory  framework in terms of boosting growth  and creating jobs particularly for young  people but many underlined the need to  maintain stability and predictability  for investors
 

